Hooks in this directory run, in filename sort order, every time
wkdmgr-mgmt successfully revokes (deletes) a key -- after the row has
already been removed from the SQLite store.

Calling convention
-------------------
    <script> <email> <domain>

- argv[1] is the full email address whose key was just revoked.
- argv[2] is the domain part of that address.
- No stdin payload: the key is already gone from the database by the
  time these hooks run, so there is nothing to pipe in.

Only files in this directory that are both regular files and marked
executable (any of the owner/group/other execute bits) are run. Files
without the execute bit -- including this README -- are ignored.
Scripts run in filename order, so prefix them (01-, 02-, ...) if order
matters.

Each hook gets a fixed timeout (default 10s, configurable via
`hook_timeout_secs` in config.yaml). A hook that times out is killed. A
hook that fails (non-zero exit) or hangs is logged as a warning and does
not affect the revocation that already happened -- hooks are a
best-effort side effect, not part of the transaction.

There are no bundled hooks in v1. This directory is the intended
extension point for later integrations (for example, removing a key
from a mail-encryption gateway's keyring), which wkdmgr's core does not
need to know anything about.
